    "result_text": "Done. The test passes (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline validates the email in its first step, `ValidateNewOwner`, so I added a disposable-domain blocklist check there and wired a dedicated 400 escalation, matching the repo's existing one-exception-per-rule pattern (`InvalidEmailException`, `MissingOwnerFieldsException`, etc.).\n\n- **`ValidateNewOwner.java`** \u2014 after the syntactic email check and lower-casing, extract the domain (everything after the last `@`) and reject it with a new `DisposableEmailException` when it's in `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS = {mailinator.com, tempmail.com, guerrillamail.com}`. Added the exception to the `throws` clause so OfficeFloor routes it as an escalation.\n- **`DisposableEmailException.java`** (new) \u2014 checked exception carrying the offending address, mirroring `InvalidEmailException`.\n- **`DisposableEmailExceptionHandler.java`** (new) \u2014 responds `400` with an RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail` (via the shared `ProblemDetails` helper) and `errors: [\"email\"]`, mirroring the invalid-email handler.\n- **`...escalation.DisposableEmailException.yml`** (new) \u2014 maps the exception to its handler.\n\nThe check runs only when an email is present (email stays optional) and compares against the canonical lower-cased domain. The blocklist lives in one named `Set`, so extending it later is a one-line change.\n\nNote: the `PUT /api/owners/{ownerId}` path uses `ValidateOwner` (bean-validation via `@Valid`), which has no custom email logic, so the disposable check doesn't run there. The spec's test covers creation only; I kept the change scoped to where email is actually processed. Say the word if you'd like the same rule enforced on update.",
