    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Exception + handler + YAML triad** mirrors the existing `InvalidEmailException` / `InvalidEmailExceptionHandler` set exactly: checked exception carrying the offending value, handler building the response through the shared `ProblemDetails.build` helper, `errors: [\"email\"]` property, and a one-line escalation YAML keyed by FQCN. A maintainer would find this immediately familiar.\n- **Rule placement is right.** The disposable-domain check sits in `ValidateNewOwner` directly after the syntactic `EMAIL_PATTERN` check, operating on the already-canonicalized (lower-cased) value. Email validation already lives inline in this class (the update path's `ValidateOwner` delegates to `@Valid`), so adding the new rule here \u2014 rather than inventing a new step or splitting it off \u2014 matches the established structure. The `throws` clause and import are extended minimally.\n- **No duplication.** The blocklist is a single `Set.of(...)` constant; domain extraction is a one-liner next to the logic that uses it. Nothing is copied across classes.\n- `ValidateNewOwner` is already the field-validation unit for owner creation; this addition stays within that responsibility rather than turning the class into something new.\n\nOne optional, non-blocking observation: this codebase extracts per-field normalization into helpers (`OwnerAddress.normalize`, `OwnerTelephone.toE164`) but keeps email handling inline. The new check follows the inline email convention, so it introduces no new inconsistency \u2014 just worth noting if email logic ever grows enough to warrant its own `OwnerEmail` helper.",
